alue" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/5243b22ebef45709f214ac2422cfb72528f24fd5ef51bd38aa3fc0ff40f1888d.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-332707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-332707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "727c6876100d08ae032659276e3b453d2d1ae8fed06910a1a96a7ea6adbd89b2"} {"question" "q-332707" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-332707" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-332707" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-332751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-332751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/96801605aa5d777eb18c13ded1f7b7e59ae74968b210da3b7a26f339a1c9f673.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{7t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{7t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{3t})^{′}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}}{3{c}_{1}{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-332751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "727c6876100d08ae032659276e3b453d2d1ae8fed06910a1a96a7ea6adbd89b2"} {"question" "q-332751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-332751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/64c0ac27236c4bfae452572e6b5b4c1d6184c45ebac9149ca4fc442d7533bb51.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/9fb1f3c545855e7bdfe591574a9069e69bdcc0cc750e5fc4f5aa3eccfdc7ea5a.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/9ff104bc583ec025ede6de68f7ea546dea99c914153b8e2325c6f2f9188d0a9b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/eceeabfeb428687bb9f630fe3578937c3fd4fb910605dc25daf56ad5ce18a68a.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-332751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-333184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-333184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/2473117cdca4318187b94374820c0a12576e998e7f631a6e7bf51e56c985be43.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{6t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{6{c}_{1}{e}^{6t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-333184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "727c6876100d08ae032659276e3b453d2d1ae8fed06910a1a96a7ea6adbd89b2"} {"question" "q-333184" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-333184" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/0b2cfbe650613293fe807be4ff7e38fa6f10b2e792dae8f7f4b78f27560f59d7.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/b930f98928e3c4af06434c720d953ad0fcee17500113ed9a72492eda4219a7fd.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/ef7ed85b18002aeec299826750ecc6306c90db8bf2c