pe/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-333214" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a saddle point"] ["text" "a stable node (sink)"] ["text" "an unstable node (source)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "q-333214" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "a saddle point"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/state.json" "file_sha256" "1788a635c8e0149bcea3899eb9366c7fa8a9fae36dac0f57f6ebf6083588e2ca"} {"question" "e-21596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/040ac76a28687db1c99d3379242b726a85aa4baf269a7c6d35d9662af1d3bd78.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/example-21596.json" "file_sha256" "19db04659d0c0763367a31be254d944f3eb5a4679fc193eb18a5c39695850e7a"} {"question" "e-21596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/2ee58da2733a468406869e43f57deebc6f7924f1d1149c0ca9391ea9995d9f87.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/83596c7f3038e585eb35ffafc966810e6891b55fc4d1efb04494bdc4b3755b05.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/af5b69c599a13a707365e88f6f50939064c7b199a1d8231b32a1c88e425440fa.png)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/833ce06c0c6d5f9beea87a84ef1b286748472c312987e837ca6f47f60225b1fa.png)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/fb015d0935718dd6ffdfb9eb13b2b8fd0c255c63a2f5cecdb988a3f83df14685.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/03f2711cb5a80e1e94f879bf8a68c25db27f8280f5e1e344425f9ee2a334a829.png)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{4t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/bd4d36cd68896e6360317a0c01c92841423020218436d85ae07eb413463fffa8.png)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-6t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/91c074766b92790a0e78b768b0290d7723617d6ed30e51e842501cad63e58a44.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/example-21596.json" "file_sha256" "19db04659d0c0763367a31be254d944f3eb5a4679fc193eb18a5c39695850e7a"} {"question" "e-21597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/25d4f77a3f596202229581e06d88a9e3bdd84dc6b8b7e14b6f46b3b3d2f125d2.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/example-21597.json" "file_sha256" "58fd2da19a17093eb82da6bc2578b85b58b09f61a69f414fad385f170ff1f4af"} {"question" "e-21597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/343f6f32ac21a1bf06b86174734dd9ebba6ddb7598cbe83b4ade59f70e8e8b26.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/cb0f32e55af289452f1429ddfe3214a46501c55f3a61deaf10b4f736e47dca7f.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/85fdb4fc83a6f2b44178be2e97a0e7985d97350deb4e0fb123e513a80090906a.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/3f984a493e976bd522455395defcb4c1562d61ca56e19fa27e2272832ce6011c.png)\nmust approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 < 0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/44f82894493c95c38039bc1e6a842bb7226571e9818e3c10c50d33627464ea37.png)\nmust also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 < 0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies $\\lambda_1 < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/example-21597.json" "file_sha256" "58fd2da19a17093eb82da6bc2578b85b58b09f61a69f414fad385f170ff1f4af"} {"question" "e-21633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/470210fb9509e87c3e51a990bac7483e5862d2f37afdce9823279838e8391592.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/example-21633.json" "file_sha256" "32a655a941ba85aa04928483898949859a45b82ecb3372649dc044abbe471954"} {"question" "e-21633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/703fb7adc0ca076e42aecc781d4d0dc58eb5f4fec03db4d18bf5a36f7055a349.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/7210186106fc202e3b9a35ffa32b06b09493ce28c191a89c2824a35a547febff.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/f292220b15ff9b0d695cb8b9f2349cc275557fd2b77c32c147e25365ab0f7e47.png)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/5671487738e750f488ad61852f17fb4b6b14cf4050fee66338000d4939914ce6.png)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/651cc55c8b96c402fc00140c06736493e71276666ce4eb1ba74c2294600a1b89.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/222dcd944fc89cd0f07970cddf5c50aa167c7f2d25b1f7305779c3dbb0a4a6af.png)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/08ad54b92f62513fb88f12f7bd6da358263e46673d686e0982a1e8044b8ab131.png)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/c5b1aa6ccde39c56b503e2d59a56e2ac8058b08495567f4e2cb40f6a056faedd.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t) = c_1e^{-t}$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{-4t}$ provided that $c_1,c_2 \\neq 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/6c3af2fe68c4bcddbbfd9f13eb84e73014511374c0adc570084f9c92773d31e0.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/example-21633.json" "file_sha256" "32a655a941ba85aa04928483898949859a45b82ecb3372649dc044abbe471954"} {"question" "e-21607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nInterpret the stability of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the equilibrium point (the origin) for the given decoupled system of linear differential equations." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/example-21607.json" "file_sha256" "9f7dabcf1d270cb3abb2f7903df7a73431e59786cc850609e3b3fcdc36389e48"} {"question" "e-21607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/7f53fdda091242e4fee1edd70a7e96c649eff8bac93fcdc9fed6da38ec5c7523.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/3b9909cba711a2394b8dbcda4b9d72ffbd9d37bd24b90506ac22b44c4cd398c4.png)\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=2$ with the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/1ea46da3e9697ee89d1112dfec53457657271c391a28a6c55267da70d38157c4.png) The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/9725d696549426d16093b7cd0e9bb188589472ed9ff81520c19a07ab296fb96d.png)\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=4$ with the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/85e8d745e2b504770815bb92ad1a151f422c941874f33840593836e78eeaacde.png) The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/2f67b02cda725daa0d0b994f3f4952065793e53ddc32260b14310f5158876f4f.png)\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{an unstable node (source)}}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/2b79201d15fe5d2a9896cdee09c0474b97bb933d75fcf478c6fdb37cb4d16742.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/example-21607.json" "file_sha256" "9f7dabcf1d270cb3abb2f7903df7a73431e59786cc850609e3b3fcdc36389e48"} {"question" "q-332717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "e198931a73707dddd89ceb0d72caade6ad9a34850563dc25cefac8453206bd7a" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "For a decoupled system x'=lambda1*x and y'=lambda2*y, inward arrows on both coordinate axes require lambda1<0 and lambda2<0. The original portrait shows both directions approaching the origin. Only original option 1 has both coefficients negative: x'=-2x and y'=-5y. The authentic worked solution makes this same deduction, and the selected option is graded Correct. The stored semicolon-delimited system contains precisely these same equations; the unresolved checker result is a serialization issue, not a contradictory key." "bindings" {"problem" "d292572bc16a20bf29791295cccd68ecf9ade6ab7bc6079e54c2dd08b96f71da" "worked_solution" "53cf01bb63222c67105f5650bc8aa5b949f257bec63523f029a378d509559a44" "original_field" "0a42a3c2327a81611f96843045f67c90749b4efd05ca5dbcfacb2847efc509db" "reviewed_field" "4d09d0bbd149f31fc484987e44eec6699f28e4b96c30e8ef0bd8be57c8b92769" "raw_widget" "79698b31d3a130a94a08eb797d428f0099a7242fff93b9fef0028cc5c53e63cf"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-332717-before.json" "sha256" "747924c0410c7441f18a298768e807983b85e174559b54804a5c01cf51a4da2a"} {"path" "q-332717-after.png" "sha256" "438191affa4916df9408a802fac9f5ea4ef23e9862b1e86390f45a23c6f9842a"} {"path" "assets/039bae0d2eedf7d12d8283b2b134855c531efcba1bd0fa14fe9f841b28d5204e.png" "sha256" "039bae0d2eedf7d12d8283b2b134855c531efcba1bd0fa14fe9f841b28d5204e"}]} {"question" "q-332717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "e198931a73707dddd89ceb0d72caade6ad9a34850563dc25cefac8453206bd7a" "rationale" "For a decoupled system x'=lambda1*x and y'=lambda2*y, inward arrows on both coordinate axes require lambda1<0 and lambda2<0. The original portrait shows both directions approaching the origin. Only original option 1 has both coefficients negative: x'=-2x and y'=-5y. The authentic worked solution makes this same deduction, and the selected option is graded Correct. The stored semicolon-delimited system contains precisely these same equations; the unresolved checker result is a serialization issue, not a contradictory key."}] "usage" {"q-332271" [] "e-21633" [] "e-21597" [] "e-21607" [] "q-332717" [] "e-21596" [] "q-332579" [] "q-331400" []} "basis" 2375 "retractions" [[17592186167135 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t); y^{′}(t) = 3y(t)$\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?"] [17592186167153 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-x(t); y^{′}(t) = 4y(t)$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?"] [17592186167155 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332717.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?"] [17592186167155 :question/answer-fields 17592186164317] [17592186167145 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t); y^{′}(t) = 3y(t)$\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?"] [17592186166468 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 4x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)$\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/3e95cfcc1b3c61d0939dcf24b15dc270.png)"] [17592186166468 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{4t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-6t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/606b8a3c79391a14d997ef7231295e1e.png)"] [17592186166469 :question/problem "Consider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/788240fc680483e8d3c84baf05680420.png)\n\n1. ${x^{′}(t) =-5x(t); y^{′}(t) = 6y(t)$\n2. ${x^{′}(t) = 5x(t); y^{′}(t) = 6y(t)$\n3. ${x^{′}(t) = 5x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)$\n4. ${x^{′}(t) =-5x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)$"] [17592186166469 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}λ_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & λ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we must have $\\lambda_1 < 0$.\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we must have $\\lambda_2 < 0$.\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies $\\lambda_1 < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) =-5x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)\n$$"] [17592186166474 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-x(t); y^{′}(t) =-4y(t)$\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/5b9c9d82e7a3509295ac890d9c2e3e29.png)"] [17592186166474 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/808f8621a6a3c0729a4f09a90e506461.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions\n\n$$\nx(t) = c_1e^{-t}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(t)=c_2e^{-4t}\n$$\n\nprovided that\n\n$$\nc_1,c_2 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = (y^{′}(t))/(x^{′}(t)) = ((c_{2}e^{-4t})^{′})/((c_{1}e^{-t})^{′}) = \\frac{-4c_{2}e^{-4t}}{-c_{1}e^{-t}} = \\frac{4c_{2}}{c_{1}}e^{-3t}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\to 0\n$$\n\nas $t \\to \\infty$, meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/8c1e97a2503b3ad40f78284d599a6a91.png)"] [17592186166472 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t); y^{′}(t) = 4y(t)$\nInterpret the stability of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the equilibrium point (the origin) for the given decoupled system of linear differential equations."] [17592186166472 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=2$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, the $x$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=4$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, the $y$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{an unstable node (source)}}$.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/fbc80d18c07900cef20d4276d4e2c539.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186164172 17592186164173 17592186164174 17592186164175 17592186164176 17592186164222 17592186164223 17592186164224 17592186164225 17592186164226 17592186164302 17592186164303 17592186164304 17592186164305 17592186164306 17592186164318 17592186164319 17592186164320 17592186164321 17592186164322] "immutable_fields" [[17592186164171 :answer-field/correct] [17592186164171 :answer-field/id] [17592186164171 :answer-field/key] [17592186164171 :answer-field/type] [17592186164221 :answer-field/correct] [17592186164221 :answer-field/id] [17592186164221 :answer-field/key] [17592186164221 :answer-field/type] [17592186164301 :answer-field/correct] [17592186164301 :answer-field/id] [17592186164301 :answer-field/key] [17592186164301 :answer-field/type] [17592186164